All the work I've put in for the past 2+ years with Python and everything computer related is paying off.
I got a fully remote position as a Network Security Automation Engineer making $93,000/yr.
(Same as my current salary but I just wanted to type that out and look at it because wow)
I'm still accepting it. i've had the news now for about 2 weeks and it still hasn't full set in i think.
of course I feel lucky, but at the same time I deserve it.
I worked so hard for this and here it is.
Now will it be everything I ever wanted? I don't expect it to be.
but i sure did want it a whole lot.
I grew stagnant where I was.
Part of the reason I got to this train of thinking was thinking about my childhood, and everthing thats happened leading to now.
I wanted to see if I can remember what I was doing year by year.
Before I met Brittany and even during the beginning of our relationship, I had grown complacent where I was.
I was making about $75k/yr, and overall just not that invested in my career.
But something changed, something sparked in about the Spring of 2024.
It was a culmination of things that resulted in me being done flatlining.
I was with Brittany and I wanted to not just impress her, but be able to provide, and prove to myself that I could do that.
But the thing that really sparked it was not being sent to the Python class that Brad and Seth got to attend.
This was after I was told that I would be able to attend that class after showing an interest.
In the back of my mind for years was Python, wanted to learn about it and use it.
I always knew it would be beneficial.
This was also a time where I had been watching PirateSoftware who had been quite motivational at the time to learn new tech things.
So I dove in hard in that Spring of 2024.
Bootcamps, courses, homelab, I wanted to learn it all not just Python but operating systems, Linux, AI, programming, networking, all the bits and bytes.
I remember Brittany saying one time "He feels lost without a project", and it was true.
I wanted to be good at what I did, not just some scrub like I was.
So I got good.
I went from literally gaming on my work laptop while I was at work, to learning basically every day.
It started with Brilliant, then coursera, boot.dev, Cisco Learning, all that I could.
And this would burn me out on occasion, but i loved it. and still do.
Summer of 2024, I remember "releasing" my first script, the famous pingsweeper (which I still use, just used it today actually).
and what a sense of accomplishment that was. I remember installing it on my laptop and using at my Aunt's just to pingsweep the network and see it work.
and what a long way I've come since then.
I kept on learning though, learning how to use restful APIs with Aruba and Cisco.
Using PowerShell to automate the AD groups we deal with.
Getting better and better and then being known as the "script guy" on the team or having that "Mackin Magic".
In a short time after learning some basic scripting and APIs, I had that reputation on my team. it was wild.
And I kept on going. trying to find things that would be beneficial to my team.
Opportunity arose in early 2025, with the E911 project.
Since I had been known as the script guy, I was basically volunteered to work on that project with Tom and CJ, who I had respected a lot.
CJ was always known as a cool switch guy and Tom was newer young blood that had a reputation of doing some cool programming/automation projects.
I was stoked actually. I had most of the data they wanted in a short time, gave ideas, all of that and it felt good.
So I worked on that all the time.
On a personal level, we were dealing with a lot around that time as well with Tommen passing. then having the fire shortly after.
But I kept it moving, still learning on coursera, still grinding.
Throughout that summer it was so busy with moving and arranging and everything.
I think it was around that time that I discovered and became addicted to boot.dev
What an amazing platform.
I was already a bit familiar with Python so I breezed through its course, and just wanted to do lessons all the time.
Grinded that till about level 70 on the platform, getting to the point in the course where I felt like it wouldn't benefit me in my job at the time.
which was kinda true, but we'll get back to boot.dev
I decided that it was time to get a certification, the CCNA.
Another career related goal that I had wanted this whole time since PTI.
It was the gold standard for an IT certification.
So I pumped the brakes on Python and scripting and hyperfocused on getting the CCNA.
I found Jeremy Ciara's (?) course and it was great, then used the Boson test sims to really practice.
I basically studied that entire November and December of 2025, passing my CCNA test in early 2026.
What another great feeling that was, I was pumped, I won't forget that.
Passed it on my first try too. Again, I deserved it.
But the grind didn't stop there...
It was back to programming, back to automation, Python, Go, bash.
Boot.dev was calling my name once again and I came right back.
I remember leaving off right around the beginning of the Go course (leaving fall-ish of 2025, returning spring or so of 2026)
It was like riding a fuckin bike man I was right back in that bitch.
Learning Go of all things, that was not on my bingo card. but it was amazing.
I remember the Chirpy project, building a backend restful API server with JWT auth, using postres, i mean who am i at this point?
It was hard lesson after hard lesson after hard lesson. back to back to back to back to back level 10 difficulty lessons.
It always starts with a blank canvas, and somehow i would just always fill the screen with code. little by little, piecing it all together until it worked.
I love doing that. the sense of purpose I feel and accomplishment when the code works.
So I got through that course, and at that point I'm realizing I can do anything in this cirriculum if I can do that.
I can do anything in tech if I can do that.
and I set my sights towards work from home.
got the interview with Netperf, seemed promising but that didn't work out.
When the Netsec Automation Engineer position opened, I was ready to jump on it. pounced even.
All of these projects, all of the learning I had done led to this point.
I nailed the interview, it was the easiest of my life. you could say i entered a flow state during the interview.
the next week I got the call while at Haubfrahaus of all places, and accepted the position.
yeah i feel lucky, but this is a case of luck where preparation meets opportunity.
So now here I am.
- homelab nerd
- CCNA
- script/api/ai/python guy reputation
- archmage on boot.dev
- fucking working from home, wow
I remember feeling like all of these things felt out of reach at some point, but in the end, I did it.
and I don't plan on stopping. maybe moving at a slower pace, or really dialing it in for Security topics coming up.
But I won't go back to being complacent.
So many great things have happened since making this decision and I'm looking forward to more.

